 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**It's probably not just you, but Perth's price rise is milder than the rest of the country's. Your bill may feel big because of the level of your prices and the end of government credits, not a sudden jump in tariffs.**\n\n**ABS CPI, electricity, annual change**\n\n| | Perth | Australia (8 capitals) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aug 2026 | **+2.4%** | **+13.2%** |\n| Jun 2026 | +2.5% | +22.4% |\n| Jan–Feb 2026 | +250% to +289% (rebate distortion) | +32% to +37% |\n\n- **Perth's underlying tariff rise is small and steady.** Prices went up 2.5% in each of the 12 months to Jun 2025 and Jun 2026, then 2.4% in Jul and Aug 2026. That's below Perth's overall inflation of 4.3% in Aug 2026.\n- **The rest of the country has had bigger rises.** Australia's electricity index rose from 94.4 in Jun 2025 to 113.7 in Aug 2026.\n- **Perth's monthly figures swing wildly.** Examples are −50% in Aug 2024 and +289% in Aug 2025. I think this comes from state government energy credits, which the CPI counts as a lower price while they're applied. That is my inference. The data doesn't label the cause. Perth's index fell to about 26 in Aug 2024 and was back at 100 by Jul 2025. When the credits finish, your bill rises sharply even though the tariff barely moved.\n- **Other costs are rising faster.** Perth's overall CPI was 4.3% in Aug 2026, above the national 4.0%. Your power bill is competing with higher prices for everything else.\n\n**Why it may still feel huge**\n1. Any credit you got on earlier bills has probably ended, so the full amount now shows.\n2. Perth's tariff has gone up about 2.5% a year, and that compounds on top of earlier increases.\n3. Your own usage matters. Winter heating, summer air-conditioning, a pool or an EV can swamp the tariff change.\n\nCheck your bill for a line showing a credit or rebate in earlier periods, and compare your daily usage in kWh with your last few bills. If usage is flat and the credit is gone, that explains it.",
